<DocsLayout
  title="Mobile"
  subtitle="An Expo client of a paired desktop. The phone does not own the database."
  description="CADCAMFUN iOS companion: pairing, pinned TLS, routes, and what is still unverified on device."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>
      WP13 on the task board is <strong>Expo migration integrated</strong>: gateway, pairing,
      the pinned local native module, and assistant resources. The board records 48 tests, a
      passing typecheck, and a passing JavaScript export. Native Release and device validation
      are in progress. TestFlight is pending. Spec 13’s later changelog line records 56 mobile
      unit tests with device Release verification still in progress. Quote the board for the
      milestone, and do not treat either count as a finished device release. Owner directory:
      <code>apps/mobile</code> (<code>@cadcamfun/mobile</code>). Spec:
      <code>specs/13-vcs-actions-mobile.md</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Expo Go cannot connect">
      <p>
        The local <code>CompanionNet</code> module needs a native development build. Expo Go can
        show a setup screen. It cannot fall back to ordinary <code>fetch</code> without the pin.
        Unsupported platforms and web do not silently replace pinning.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="layout" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Layout</h2>
    <p>
      React Native and Expo Router. The old Tauri mobile shell is gone. Do not restore it.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Path</th><th>Role</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>apps/mobile/app/</code></td><td><code>_layout.tsx</code>, <code>index.tsx</code>, <code>connect.tsx</code>, <code>pair.tsx</code>, and <code>(app)/projects</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>apps/mobile/lib/pairing.ts</code></td><td>QR, paste, deep link, manual address. Races private IPv4s from the QR.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>apps/mobile/lib/client.ts</code>, <code>native.ts</code>, <code>sse.ts</code></td><td>HTTP client, optional native-module load, SSE parsing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>apps/mobile/lib/storage.ts</code>, <code>store.ts</code>, <code>offline-queue.ts</code></td><td>SecureStore for pairing credentials. Offline assistant queue.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>apps/mobile/modules/companion-net/</code></td><td>Local Expo module. iOS implementation pins the leaf certificate.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>apps/mobile/components/</code></td><td><code>chat</code>, <code>layout</code>, <code>project</code>, <code>ui</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>apps/mobile/hooks/</code></td><td><code>use-host-resource.ts</code>, <code>use-copied-feedback.ts</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Routes cover pairing, projects, history, pull requests, Actions, the assistant, activity,
      and settings. <code>cadcamfun://pair</code> opens the pairing route and does not put the
      device token in the UI or in logs. Reconnect keeps credentials when the desktop is offline,
      checks health without overlapping probes, and cancels stale resource and chat requests
      when the client, project, or screen changes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="transport" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Transport</h2>
    <p>
      The sidecar API listens on <code>127.0.0.1</code>. The phone reaches it through the
      companion gateway in <code>apps/sidecar/src/services/companion-gateway.ts</code> (and
      <code>companion.ts</code>). The desktop turns it on at Settings, Mobile companion
      (<code>/settings/companion</code>). It is off by default and comes back after a restart
      only if it was on.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gateway is a separate HTTPS listener on <code>0.0.0.0</code>, default port 8443, with
      a per-install self-signed P-256 certificate under <code>&lt;dataDir&gt;/companion/</code>
      (key mode 0600). The QR carries the certificate SHA-256 fingerprint. The phone pins that
      fingerprint. There is no trust-on-first-use and no cleartext HTTP. Device tokens are 32
      random bytes. Only their SHA-256 is stored, compared in constant time, and shown once
      inside the QR. The gateway swaps in the sidecar’s real bearer token when it forwards, so
      the phone never holds that token. Revoking a device on the desktop cuts it off on the next
      request. Ten failed authentications per minute from one address return 429.
    </p>
    <p>
      iOS code is <code>modules/companion-net/ios/CompanionNetModule.swift</code>: ephemeral
      URLSession, pinned leaf certificate, HTTPS only, incremental UTF-8 and SSE. Redirects and
      cross-origin paths are refused. Cancellation closes the task.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Allowed</th><th>Refused (403)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>GET /api/health</code>, <code>GET /api/projects</code>, project vcs / pulls / actions reads</td><td>Credentials, plugins, files, billing, <code>/api/companion</code> admin</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>GET /api/ai/status</code>, <code>/models</code>, <code>/agents</code></td><td>Provider mutation, skills mutation, host configuration</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /api/ai/chat/stream</code>, abort, queue, permission reply, session control and state</td><td><code>POST /vcs/push</code> and <code>/vcs/pull</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The pairing QR lists every private IPv4 (Wi-Fi, personal hotspot, Tailscale). The phone
      races them and warms local-network permission so a scan on cellular is not dropped. A
      private overlay network works when the phone can route to the desktop: the pin and the
      device token stay the same. A hosted relay is not built. If one is added later it has to
      carry the TLS stream end to end.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="assistant" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Assistant</h2>
    <p>
      The phone uses the same server-owned CAD/CAM assistant as the desktop. It can propose.
      It commits on <code>ai/*</code> and opens a pull request. A person merges.
      <code>companion-chat-e2e.test.ts</code> boots a real sidecar, points MiniMax at a scripted
      OpenAI-compatible server, pairs over pinned TLS, and checks that <code>main</code> still
      holds only the human commit. That test proves the plumbing and the guards. It does not
      prove the quality of a real model.
    </p>
    <p>
      The transcript is full width, with inspectable CAD, CAM, VCS, and Actions tool cards.
      The composer can steer during a run. Specialist selection is read-only through
      <code>GET /api/ai/agents</code>. Offline, the assistant queues and drains in the
      foreground. Settings shows a connection pill and the queued count. Biometrics live in
      <code>lib/biometrics.ts</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="commands" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Commands</h2>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="From apps/mobile">
{`bun run ios          # native app + Metro
bun run ios:build     # build without Metro
bun run ios:dev       # Metro for an installed dev build
bun dev               # Metro for the development client`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>expo dev</code> is not an Expo CLI command. iOS simulator verification and a signed
      device or TestFlight build are separate outcomes. <code>.github/workflows/ios.yml</code>
      generates the Expo iOS project and builds an unsigned Release simulator app. The workflow
      file is not proof that CI has gone green. Device signing needs the owner’s Apple team and
      certificates. Push notifications (APNs) and a hosted relay remain separate work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Local checks recorded in the spec: unit tests, TypeScript, and the iOS JavaScript export.
      Record a device Release only after that build actually runs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Boundaries</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The phone must not ship a second copy of the project database or spawn the Bun sidecar.</li>
      <li>Transport must stay certificate-pinned HTTPS. Do not add an unpinned fetch fallback.</li>
      <li>The device token and the desktop bearer token must not appear in UI or logs.</li>
      <li>The gateway must keep the route allow-list. Credentials, plugins, billing, and GitHub push stay on the desktop.</li>
      <li>The assistant on the phone must not merge to <code>main</code>.</li>
      <li>TestFlight and a passing device Release are still outstanding. Do not describe the companion as shipped.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
